        <!-- 3.9: DEEP DIVE EACH -->
        <div id="types-each" class="doc-section">
          <h3 class="doc-h3">3.9. Deep Dive: Slicing &amp; Restacking with <code>each</code></h3>
          <p class="doc-p">
            The each operator is Morflow's parallel map-and-restack construct. It dynamically slices a multi-dimensional payload across a specific axis, dispatches each slice independently across CPU workers, and deterministically restacks the transformed slices back together in original sequence order.
          </p>

          <div class="doc-callout" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); background: rgba(59, 130, 246, 0.05); padding: 16px 20px; margin-bottom: 24px; border-radius: 6px;">
            <strong style="font-size: 15px; color: var(--grey-900);">The Cardinal Rule of each: Higher-Rank Type Restacking</strong>
            <p class="doc-p" style="margin-top: 8px; margin-bottom: 0;">
              The foundational invariant governing each is: <strong>the result is always stacked back to the exact same data type as the input</strong>. Because Morflow automatically reassembles the aggregated slices into the original higher-rank container, <strong>the result of each sub-flow must be able to be combined back into the original higher-rank type</strong>.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h4 class="doc-h3" style="font-size: 16px; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 12px;">Slicing &amp; Restacking Semantics by Type</h4>
          <div class="doc-table-container">
            <table class="doc-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Incoming Higher-Rank Payload</th>
                  <th>Iteration Axis</th>
                  <th>Sub-Flow Slice (<code>$var</code>)</th>
                  <th>Required Sub-Flow Output</th>
                  <th>Restacked Result</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>Tensor[rank=N]</code> (N &ge; 2)</td>
                  <td>Axis 0 (Batch / Leading)</td>
                  <td><code>Tensor[rank=N-1]</code></td>
                  <td><code>Tensor[rank=N-1]</code></td>
                  <td><code>Tensor[rank=N]</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>Tensor[rank=1]</code> (1D Vector)</td>
                  <td>Axis 0 (Elements)</td>
                  <td><code>Scalar</code></td>
                  <td><code>Scalar</code></td>
                  <td><code>Tensor[rank=1]</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>Image[H, W, C]</code> (HWC layout)</td>
                  <td>Axis 2 (Channel Axis)</td>
                  <td><code>Image[H, W, 1]</code> (Grayscale)</td>
                  <td><code>Image[H, W, 1]</code></td>
                  <td><code>Image[H, W, C]</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>Image[C, H, W]</code> (CHW layout)</td>
                  <td>Axis 0 (Channel Axis)</td>
                  <td><code>Image[1, H, W]</code> (Grayscale)</td>
                  <td><code>Image[1, H, W]</code></td>
                  <td><code>Image[C, H, W]</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>Audio[C, S]</code> (C &gt; 1, Planar)</td>
                  <td>Axis 0 (Channel Axis)</td>
                  <td><code>Audio[1, S]</code> (Mono Audio)</td>
                  <td><code>Audio[1, S]</code> (Mono Audio)</td>
                  <td><code>Audio[C, S]</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>Audio[1, S]</code> (Mono Audio)</td>
                  <td>Axis 0 (Sample Axis)</td>
                  <td><code>Scalar</code> (Float sample)</td>
                  <td><code>Scalar</code></td>
                  <td><code>Audio[1, S]</code></td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h4 class="doc-h3" style="font-size: 16px; margin-top: 24px; margin-bottom: 12px;">Higher-Rank Restacking Contracts</h4>
          <p class="doc-p">
            Morflow's execution engine enforces strict type and dimension symmetry when reassembling sub-flow results:
          </p>
          <ul class="doc-list" style="margin: 0 0 16px 20px; color: var(--grey-800); line-height: 1.65;">
            <li>
              <strong>Higher-Rank Images (Channel Reassembly):</strong> When looping over a multi-channel Image, each parallel iteration receives a single grayscale channel. The sub-flow must output a single-channel Image preserving spatial height and width (H &times; W). When all channel sub-flows conclude, the engine stacks them along the channel axis (Axis 2 in HWC, Axis 0 in CHW) and restores the original ColorSpace (e.g. RGB) and layout. The checker validates payload-kind compatibility, and the runtime verifies channel shapes during reassembly.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Higher-Rank Audio (Multi-Channel Synchronization):</strong> Slicing a multi-channel Audio yields mono Audio streams. Each branch must produce a mono Audio with identical sample rates and layouts. Morflow stacks the processed mono streams along the channel axis back into the original multi-channel higher-rank Audio container (transposing planar buffers into interleaved buffers if the parent audio used interleaved layout). When mono Audio is sliced per-sample, single Scalar float samples are processed and stacked back into mono Audio.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Higher-Rank Tensors (Axis 0 Concatenation):</strong> Slicing a rank-N Tensor along Axis 0 produces rank-(N &minus; 1) slices. Every parallel branch must yield a rank-(N &minus; 1) Tensor with identical inner dimension sizes. The runtime stacks them back along Axis 0 to recreate the higher-rank rank-N Tensor.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h4 class="doc-h3" style="font-size: 16px; margin-top: 24px; margin-bottom: 12px;">The Core Rules of <code>each</code></h4>
          <ul class="doc-list" style="margin: 0 0 16px 20px; color: var(--grey-800); line-height: 1.65;">
            <li>
              <strong>1. The Higher-Rank Restacking Contract (E009):</strong> The sub-flow inside each must preserve the payload kind of the loop variable. Because the engine restacks outputs back into the higher-rank container of the input, the result of each sub-flow must be combinable back into the original higher-rank type. If an Image channel loop yields a Tensor or Scalar, or an Audio loop yields non-audio data, compilation halts with E009: 'each' body changes the payload kind.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>2. Emitting Loop Results:</strong> Slices execute concurrently across CPU worker threads. Their results are collected in iteration order, restacked, and emitted by the enclosing top-level flow.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>3. Single Static Assignment (SSA) Safety:</strong> The loop variable (e.g. $channel) is immutable within its iteration scope. Intermediate variables tapped inside an each loop cannot conflict with external flows.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>4. External Parameter Access:</strong> Sub-flows inside each can freely reference external variables and configuration arguments declared in the outer scope (e.g. $channel &gt;&gt; biquad_filter(freq=$cutoff)).
            </li>
          </ul>

          <div class="doc-code-card">
            <div class="doc-code-header">
              <span>Valid vs Invalid each Sub-flows</span>
              <span>morf</span>
            </div>
            <pre class="doc-code-pre"><code><span class="tok-comment"># ❌ COMPILE ERROR E009: Sub-flow changes payload kind</span>
<span class="tok-comment"># The loop input is an Image, so each sub-flow must produce an Image.</span>
<span class="tok-comment"># Here, 'relu' returns a Tensor, which cannot be restacked back into the higher-rank Image!</span>
<span class="tok-var">$rgb_image</span>
    <span class="tok-op">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="tok-keyword">each</span> (<span class="tok-var">$channel</span>) &#123;
        <span class="tok-var">$channel</span>
            <span class="tok-op">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="tok-fn">to_tensor</span>
            <span class="tok-op">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="tok-fn">relu</span>  <span class="tok-comment"># Error E009: body produces Tensor, expected Image</span>
    &#125;

<span class="tok-comment"># ✅ VALID: Sub-flow produces compatible Image slices</span>
<span class="tok-comment"># Every slice remains an Image and restacks cleanly back into RGB Image</span>
<span class="tok-var">$rgb_image</span>
    <span class="tok-op">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="tok-keyword">each</span> (<span class="tok-var">$channel</span>) &#123;
        <span class="tok-var">$channel</span>
            <span class="tok-op">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="tok-fn">gaussian_blur</span>(<span class="tok-param">sigma</span>=<span class="tok-num">1.2</span>)
            <span class="tok-op">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="tok-fn">threshold</span>(<span class="tok-param">cutoff</span>=<span class="tok-num">128</span>)
    &#125;
    <span class="tok-op">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="tok-fn">emit</span></code></pre>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-code-card">
            <div class="doc-code-header">
              <span>Parallel Multi-Channel Audio Loop</span>
              <span>morf</span>
            </div>
            <pre class="doc-code-pre"><code><span class="tok-keyword">import</span> audio_essentials/latest

<span class="tok-keyword">accept</span> <span class="tok-type">Audio</span> <span class="tok-var">$stereo_audio</span>
<span class="tok-keyword">accept</span> <span class="tok-type">FloatArg</span> <span class="tok-var">$cutoff_freq</span> = <span class="tok-num">120.0</span>

<span class="tok-comment"># Stereo audio ($stereo_audio has 2 channels)</span>
<span class="tok-comment"># 'each' slices the channel axis, processing 2 channels in parallel</span>
<span class="tok-var">$stereo_audio</span>
    <span class="tok-op">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="tok-keyword">each</span> (<span class="tok-var">$channel</span>) &#123;
        <span class="tok-comment"># $channel is a mono Audio payload</span>
        <span class="tok-var">$channel</span>
            <span class="tok-op">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="tok-fn">biquad_filter</span>(<span class="tok-param">type</span>=<span class="tok-str">"highpass"</span>, <span class="tok-param">freq</span>=<span class="tok-var">$cutoff_freq</span>)
            <span class="tok-op">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="tok-fn">compressor</span>(<span class="tok-param">threshold_db</span>=<span class="tok-num">-12.0</span>, <span class="tok-param">ratio</span>=<span class="tok-num">3.0</span>)
    &#125;
    <span class="tok-comment"># Processed channels are restacked into stereo Audio</span>
    <span class="tok-op">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="tok-fn">stereo_widen</span>(<span class="tok-param">width</span>=<span class="tok-num">1.2</span>)
    <span class="tok-op">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="tok-fn">to_wav</span>
    <span class="tok-op">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="tok-fn">emit</span></code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div id="types-shapechecking" class="doc-section">
          <h3 class="doc-h3">3.10. Shapes &amp; Shape Checking</h3>
          <p class="doc-p">Morflow can catch incompatible shapes before you run a pipeline. For example, a tensor with 2 rows and 3 columns has six elements, so reshaping it to 3 rows and 2 columns is valid. Reshaping it to 4 rows and 2 columns is an error.</p>
          <p class="doc-p">Tell Morflow what to expect by adding a shape to your input declaration. You can specify every dimension, leave some dimensions flexible, or omit the shape entirely:</p>
          <div class="doc-table-container"><table class="doc-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Input declaration</th><th>What it accepts</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>accept Tensor $x</code></td><td>A tensor of any shape.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>accept Tensor[rank=2] $x</code></td><td>A matrix with any number of rows and columns.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>accept Tensor[*,3] $x</code></td><td>A matrix with any number of rows and exactly 3 columns.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>accept Tensor[2,3] $x</code></td><td>A matrix with exactly 2 rows and 3 columns.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p class="doc-p">Use <code>*</code> for a dimension whose size can change between runs. A size of <code>0</code> means that dimension is empty.</p>
          <p class="doc-p">When you run <code>morflow check</code>, Morflow follows the shape through each action and checks whether the next action can accept it. The more dimensions you specify, the more Morflow can verify ahead of time. Flexible dimensions still carry useful information: with <code>Tensor[*,3]</code>, Morflow knows that every row passed to <code>each</code> contains 3 elements.</p>
          <p class="doc-p">The same checking applies to Image and Audio dimensions. For a Composite, Morflow tracks each payload separately and in order. For example, <code>Composite[Tensor[2,3], Tensor[3,4]]</code> gives <code>matmul</code> a 2×3 left matrix and a 3×4 right matrix. Selecting <code>$parts[0]</code> gives you the first matrix, with its 2×3 shape.</p>
          <div class="doc-table-container"><table class="doc-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Check result</th><th>Meaning</th><th>CLI behavior</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>Verified</code></td><td>The action can accept the specified type, shape, and arguments.</td><td>Counts as verified.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>Runtime check</code></td><td>More information is needed, such as the actual input size or an argument supplied when the pipeline runs.</td><td>Shows which action needs more information and why.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>Invalid</code></td><td>The specified input or arguments do not meet the action’s requirements.</td><td>Reports an error.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p class="doc-p">Run <code>morflow check pipeline.morf</code> to see how much of your pipeline’s shape compatibility can be verified before execution. If 8 of 10 actions are verified, the report shows <strong>80% shape safety</strong>. It also shows the input and output shapes for each action and explains what is still needed for any action marked Runtime check.</p>
          <p class="doc-p">Actions in every flow, branch, and loop are included. Each action written in the pipeline counts once, even if a loop runs it many times. <code>emit</code> does not count. If there are no processing actions, the percentage is N/A.</p>
          <div class="doc-code-card"><div class="doc-code-header"><span>Concrete versus dynamic shapes</span><span>morf</span></div><pre class="doc-code-pre"><code><span class="tok-keyword">from</span> tensor_essentials/latest <span class="tok-keyword">import</span> <span class="tok-fn">reshape</span>
<span class="tok-keyword">accept</span> <span class="tok-type">Tensor[2,3]</span> <span class="tok-var">$fixed</span>
<span class="tok-keyword">accept</span> <span class="tok-type">Tensor[*,3]</span> <span class="tok-var">$dynamic</span>
<span class="tok-var">$fixed</span> <span class="tok-op">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="tok-fn">reshape</span>(<span class="tok-str">"3,2"</span>) <span class="tok-op">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="tok-fn">emit</span>(<span class="tok-str">"fixed"</span>)
<span class="tok-var">$dynamic</span> <span class="tok-op">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="tok-fn">reshape</span>(<span class="tok-str">"3,2"</span>) <span class="tok-op">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="tok-fn">emit</span>(<span class="tok-str">"dynamic"</span>)

<span class="tok-comment"># Shape safety: 50.0% (1/2 actions verified)</span>
<span class="tok-comment"># Flow 1: Verified — six input elements match six output elements.</span>
<span class="tok-comment"># Flow 2: Runtime check — the wildcard length is resolved at runtime.</span></code></pre></div>
          <p class="doc-p">In this example, the first reshape is verified because the input has exactly six elements. The second needs the actual row count before Morflow can know whether the reshape will work. You can keep that flexibility or specify the row count to make the check more precise. Arguments supplied by the caller also remain flexible, even when they have defaults.</p>
          <p class="doc-p">When you run the pipeline, Morflow checks each action using the actual input dimensions and arguments, then verifies the shape of its output. A 100% report means every action’s shape requirements have been verified for your input declarations. Requirements that depend on the data itself, such as whether a matrix can be inverted, are checked during execution.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 4: PIPELINE EXECUTION -->
      <section id="exec" class="doc-section">
        <div class="doc-section-header">
          <p class="badge">Section 4</p>
          <h2 class="doc-h2">4. Pipeline Execution</h2>
          <p class="doc-p">
            Execute the same .morf pipelines and native actions across training and production serving. Morflow provides host SDKs for Python, JavaScript/Node.js, Java, and Rust, converting host tensors, image arrays, and raw bytes into engine payloads.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- 4.1: PYTHON -->
        <div id="exec-python" class="doc-section">
          <h3 class="doc-h3">4.1. Python</h3>
          <p class="doc-p">
            The Python SDK (morflow) converts NumPy arrays and raw bytes into engine payloads and executes pipelines in Rust with the Python GIL released. Payloads can be passed as typed wrappers (morflow.Image, morflow.Audio, morflow.Tensor), or plain NumPy arrays and bytes matching declared accept types.
          </p>

          <div class="doc-code-card">
            <div class="doc-code-header">
              <span>Installation</span>
              <span>Shell</span>
            </div>
            <pre class="doc-code-pre"><code>pip install morflow</code></pre>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-code-card">
            <div class="doc-code-header">
              <span>Typed Execution &amp; Positional Arguments in Python</span>
              <span>python</span>
            </div>
            <pre class="doc-code-pre"><code><span class="tok-keyword">import</span> numpy <span class="tok-keyword">as</span> np
<span class="tok-keyword">from</span> PIL <span class="tok-keyword">import</span> Image
<span class="tok-keyword">import</span> morflow

<span class="tok-comment"># 1. Load pipeline from file</span>
pipeline = morflow.<span class="tok-fn">load</span>(<span class="tok-str">"image_pipeline.morf"</span>)

<span class="tok-comment"># 2. Pass NumPy array wrapped as morflow.Image</span>
img = Image.<span class="tok-fn">open</span>(<span class="tok-str">"input.png"</span>).<span class="tok-fn">convert</span>(<span class="tok-str">"RGB"</span>)
np_img = np.<span class="tok-fn">array</span>(img, dtype=np.uint8)

<span class="tok-comment"># 3. Execute with positional arguments (payload, target_w, target_h)</span>
<span class="tok-comment"># Declared defaults (512, 512) apply if arguments are omitted: pipeline.run(payload)</span>
result_np = pipeline.<span class="tok-fn">run</span>(morflow.<span class="tok-fn">Image</span>(np_img, color=<span class="tok-str">"rgb"</span>), <span class="tok-num">512</span>, <span class="tok-num">512</span>)

<span class="tok-comment"># 4. Save result</span>
out_img = Image.<span class="tok-fn">fromarray</span>(result_np)
out_img.<span class="tok-fn">save</span>(<span class="tok-str">"output.png"</span>)

<span class="tok-comment"># ----------------------------------------------------</span>
<span class="tok-comment"># Audio Processing (Pass and receive raw WAV bytes)</span>
<span class="tok-comment"># ----------------------------------------------------</span>
audio_pipe = morflow.<span class="tok-fn">load</span>(<span class="tok-str">"audio_pipeline.morf"</span>)
<span class="tok-keyword">with</span> <span class="tok-fn">open</span>(<span class="tok-str">"input.wav"</span>, <span class="tok-str">"rb"</span>) <span class="tok-keyword">as</span> f:
    input_bytes = f.<span class="tok-fn">read</span>()

<span class="tok-comment"># Pass raw bytes (bound to accept Bytes) and custom sample rate IntArg</span>
output_wav_bytes = audio_pipe.<span class="tok-fn">run</span>(input_bytes, <span class="tok-num">44100</span>)
<span class="tok-keyword">with</span> <span class="tok-fn">open</span>(<span class="tok-str">"output.wav"</span>, <span class="tok-str">"wb"</span>) <span class="tok-keyword">as</span> f:
    f.<span class="tok-fn">write</span>(output_wav_bytes)</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 4.2: JAVASCRIPT / NODE.JS -->
        <div id="exec-js" class="doc-section">
          <h3 class="doc-h3">4.2. JavaScript / Node.js</h3>
          <p class="doc-p">
            The Node.js SDK provides asynchronous execution (via pipeline.run() or pipeline.runArgs() offloading work to a background worker pool) and synchronous execution (via pipeline.runSync() / pipeline.runSyncArgs()). Positional arguments matching declared accept statements can be passed directly as heterogeneous arguments (numbers, booleans, strings, Buffers, or TensorInput).
          </p>

          <div class="doc-code-card">
            <div class="doc-code-header">
              <span>Installation</span>
              <span>Shell</span>
            </div>
            <pre class="doc-code-pre"><code>npm install morflow</code></pre>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-code-card">
            <div class="doc-code-header">
              <span>Async Pipeline Execution with Arguments in Node.js</span>
              <span>javascript</span>
            </div>
            <pre class="doc-code-pre"><code><span class="tok-keyword">import</span> fs <span class="tok-keyword">from</span> <span class="tok-str">'node:fs'</span>;
<span class="tok-keyword">import</span> morflow <span class="tok-keyword">from</span> <span class="tok-str">'morflow'</span>;

<span class="tok-comment">// 1. Load pipeline from file</span>
<span class="tok-keyword">const</span> pipeline = morflow.<span class="tok-fn">load</span>(<span class="tok-str">'image_pipeline.morf'</span>);

<span class="tok-comment">// 2. Construct TensorInput with explicit payloadType</span>
<span class="tok-keyword">const</span> inputBuffer = fs.<span class="tok-fn">readFileSync</span>(<span class="tok-str">'input_raw_rgb.bin'</span>);
<span class="tok-keyword">const</span> tensorInput = &#123;
  data: inputBuffer,
  shape: [<span class="tok-num">1080</span>, <span class="tok-num">1920</span>, <span class="tok-num">3</span>],
  dtype: <span class="tok-str">'u8'</span>,
  payloadType: <span class="tok-str">'image'</span>,
  colorSpace: <span class="tok-str">'rgb'</span>
&#125;;

<span class="tok-comment">// 3. Run asynchronously on worker pool with positional arguments (runArgs)</span>
<span class="tok-keyword">const</span> outputTensor = <span class="tok-keyword">await</span> pipeline.<span class="tok-fn">runArgs</span>(tensorInput, <span class="tok-num">512</span>, <span class="tok-num">512</span>);

console.<span class="tok-fn">log</span>(<span class="tok-str">`Shape: $&#123;outputTensor.shape&#125;, Dtype: $&#123;outputTensor.dtype&#125;`</span>);
<span class="tok-keyword">const</span> outBuffer = outputTensor.<span class="tok-fn">toBuffer</span>();
fs.<span class="tok-fn">writeFileSync</span>(<span class="tok-str">'output_raw_rgba.bin'</span>, outBuffer);

<span class="tok-comment">// ----------------------------------------------------</span>
<span class="tok-comment">// Audio DSP Execution (Direct Buffer &amp; Argument I/O)</span>
<span class="tok-comment">// ----------------------------------------------------</span>
<span class="tok-keyword">const</span> audioPipe = morflow.<span class="tok-fn">load</span>(<span class="tok-str">'audio_pipeline.morf'</span>);
<span class="tok-keyword">const</span> wavBuffer = fs.<span class="tok-fn">readFileSync</span>(<span class="tok-str">'input.wav'</span>);

<span class="tok-comment">// Execute asynchronously with buffer and sample rate argument</span>
<span class="tok-keyword">const</span> outWav = <span class="tok-keyword">await</span> audioPipe.<span class="tok-fn">runArgs</span>(wavBuffer, <span class="tok-num">44100</span>);
fs.<span class="tok-fn">writeFileSync</span>(<span class="tok-str">'output.wav'</span>, outWav.<span class="tok-fn">toBuffer</span>());</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 4.3: JAVA -->
        <div id="exec-java" class="doc-section">
          <h3 class="doc-h3">4.3. Java</h3>
          <p class="doc-p">
            The Java SDK wraps native JNI bindings with type-safe classes, automatic memory cleanup, and direct tensor buffers for high-throughput enterprise JVM backends. Positional parameters are bound using pipeline.runArgs(...).
          </p>

          <div class="doc-code-card">
            <div class="doc-code-header">
              <span>Maven Dependency (pom.xml)</span>
              <span>XML</span>
            </div>
            <pre class="doc-code-pre"><code>&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.morflow&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;morflow&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;0.2.0&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-code-card">
            <div class="doc-code-header">
              <span>Pipeline Execution in Java</span>
              <span>java</span>
            </div>
            <pre class="doc-code-pre"><code><span class="tok-keyword">import</span> org.morflow.Morflow;
<span class="tok-keyword">import</span> org.morflow.Pipeline;
<span class="tok-keyword">import</span> org.morflow.MorflowTensor;
<span class="tok-keyword">import</span> java.nio.file.Files;
<span class="tok-keyword">import</span> java.nio.file.Path;

<span class="tok-keyword">public class</span> <span class="tok-fn">MorflowRunner</span> &#123;
    <span class="tok-keyword">public static void</span> <span class="tok-fn">main</span>(String[] args) <span class="tok-keyword">throws</span> Exception &#123;
        <span class="tok-comment">// 1. Load pipeline</span>
        <span class="tok-keyword">try</span> (Pipeline pipeline = Morflow.<span class="tok-fn">load</span>(<span class="tok-str">"image_pipeline.morf"</span>)) &#123;
            <span class="tok-comment">// 2. Wrap image bytes into MorflowTensor [H, W, C]</span>
            <span class="tok-keyword">byte</span>[] rgbBytes = Files.<span class="tok-fn">readAllBytes</span>(Path.<span class="tok-fn">of</span>(<span class="tok-str">"input_rgb.raw"</span>));
            MorflowTensor inputTensor = MorflowTensor.<span class="tok-fn">fromByteArray</span>(
                rgbBytes,
                <span class="tok-keyword">new int</span>[]&#123;<span class="tok-num">1080</span>, <span class="tok-num">1920</span>, <span class="tok-num">3</span>&#125;
            );

            <span class="tok-comment">// 3. Execute pipeline with positional arguments (runArgs)</span>
            <span class="tok-keyword">try</span> (MorflowTensor outputTensor = pipeline.<span class="tok-fn">runArgs</span>(inputTensor, <span class="tok-num">512</span>, <span class="tok-num">512</span>)) &#123;
                <span class="tok-keyword">int</span>[] outShape = outputTensor.<span class="tok-fn">getShape</span>();
                <span class="tok-keyword">byte</span>[] outBytes = outputTensor.<span class="tok-fn">toByteArray</span>();
                Files.<span class="tok-fn">write</span>(Path.<span class="tok-fn">of</span>(<span class="tok-str">"output.raw"</span>), outBytes);
            &#125;
        &#125;

        <span class="tok-comment">// Audio WAV execution with raw byte array and sample rate argument</span>
        <span class="tok-keyword">try</span> (Pipeline audioPipe = Morflow.<span class="tok-fn">load</span>(<span class="tok-str">"audio_pipeline.morf"</span>)) &#123;
            <span class="tok-keyword">byte</span>[] inputWav = Files.<span class="tok-fn">readAllBytes</span>(Path.<span class="tok-fn">of</span>(<span class="tok-str">"input.wav"</span>));
            MorflowTensor audioOut = audioPipe.<span class="tok-fn">runArgs</span>(inputWav, <span class="tok-num">44100</span>);
            Files.<span class="tok-fn">write</span>(Path.<span class="tok-fn">of</span>(<span class="tok-str">"output.wav"</span>), audioOut.<span class="tok-fn">toByteArray</span>());
        &#125;
    &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 4.4: RUST -->
        <div id="exec-rust" class="doc-section">
          <h3 class="doc-h3">4.4. Rust</h3>
          <p class="doc-p">
            The native Rust engine crate (morflow) provides direct access to Payload variants (Image, Audio, Tensor, Data, Scalar) for native pipeline integration.
          </p>

          <div class="doc-code-card">
            <div class="doc-code-header">
              <span>Cargo Dependencies</span>
              <span>Shell</span>
            </div>
            <pre class="doc-code-pre"><code>cargo add morflow</code></pre>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-code-card">
            <div class="doc-code-header">
              <span>Native Pipeline Execution in Rust</span>
              <span>rust</span>
            </div>
            <pre class="doc-code-pre"><code><span class="tok-keyword">use</span> core_types::&#123;ColorSpace, Image, Payload, RVec&#125;;
<span class="tok-keyword">use</span> morflow::Morflow;
<span class="tok-keyword">use</span> std::fs;

<span class="tok-keyword">fn</span> <span class="tok-fn">main</span>() -&gt; Result&lt;(), Box&lt;<span class="tok-keyword">dyn</span> std::error::Error&gt;&gt; &#123;
    <span class="tok-comment">// 1. Load pipeline from file</span>
    <span class="tok-keyword">let mut</span> pipeline = Morflow::<span class="tok-fn">load</span>(<span class="tok-str">"image_pipeline.morf"</span>)?;

    <span class="tok-comment">// 2. Wrap image buffer into a Morflow Image payload</span>
    <span class="tok-keyword">let</span> img = image::<span class="tok-fn">open</span>(<span class="tok-str">"input.png"</span>)?.<span class="tok-fn">to_rgb8</span>();
    <span class="tok-keyword">let</span> input = Image::<span class="tok-fn">from_u8_hwc</span>(
        &amp;img,
        img.<span class="tok-fn">width</span>() <span class="tok-keyword">as</span> usize,
        img.<span class="tok-fn">height</span>() <span class="tok-keyword">as</span> usize,
        ColorSpace::Rgb,
    ).<span class="tok-fn">map_err</span>(|e| e.<span class="tok-fn">to_string</span>())?;

    <span class="tok-comment">// 3. Execute Morflow pipeline with declared payload</span>
    <span class="tok-keyword">let</span> outputs = pipeline.<span class="tok-fn">run</span>(Payload::Image(input))?;

    <span class="tok-comment">// 4. Extract emitted payload and save output</span>
    <span class="tok-keyword">if let</span> Payload::Image(out) = outputs.<span class="tok-fn">into_single</span>()? &#123;
        <span class="tok-keyword">let</span> bytes = out.<span class="tok-fn">to_contiguous_bytes</span>();
        image::<span class="tok-fn">save_buffer</span>(
            <span class="tok-str">"output.png"</span>,
            bytes.<span class="tok-fn">as_slice</span>(),
            out.<span class="tok-fn">width</span>() <span class="tok-keyword">as</span> u32,
            out.<span class="tok-fn">height</span>() <span class="tok-keyword">as</span> u32,
            image::ColorType::Rgba8,
        )?;
    &#125;

    <span class="tok-comment">// ----------------------------------------------------</span>
    <span class="tok-comment">// Audio DSP Execution (Direct Data Payload)</span>
    <span class="tok-comment">// ----------------------------------------------------</span>
    <span class="tok-keyword">let mut</span> audio_pipe = Morflow::<span class="tok-fn">load</span>(<span class="tok-str">"audio_pipeline.morf"</span>)?;
    <span class="tok-keyword">let</span> wav_bytes = fs::<span class="tok-fn">read</span>(<span class="tok-str">"input.wav"</span>)?;

    <span class="tok-keyword">let</span> audio_out = audio_pipe.<span class="tok-fn">run</span>(Payload::Data &#123;
        buffer: RVec::<span class="tok-fn">from</span>(wav_bytes),
    &#125;)?;

    <span class="tok-keyword">if let</span> Payload::Data &#123; buffer &#125; = audio_out.into_single()? &#123;
        fs::write(<span class="tok-str">"output.wav"</span>, buffer.as_slice())?;
    &#125;

    Ok(())
&#125;</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section id="custom-action" class="doc-section">
        <div class="doc-section-header"><p class="badge">Section 5</p><h2 class="doc-h2">5. Custom Action</h2>
          <p class="doc-p">A custom action is a Rust shared library that you import and call from a .morf pipeline. You describe which payloads it accepts, check the input shape and arguments, and implement the transformation. Morflow calls these functions for you, including the shape check before each execution.</p>
        </div>
        <div id="custom-action-build" class="doc-section"><h3 class="doc-h3">5.1. Create an Action</h3>
          <p class="doc-p">In a Morflow checkout, create <code>actions/custom_pack/custom_action/Cargo.toml</code> and <code>actions/custom_pack/custom_action/src/lib.rs</code>. The folder <code>custom_pack</code> is the pack name; the Cargo package name <code>custom_action</code> is the action name. The Cargo version is the version you will import.</p>
          <div class="doc-code-card"><div class="doc-code-header"><span>Cargo.toml</span><span>toml</span></div><pre class="doc-code-pre"><code>[<span class="tok-param">package</span>]
<span class="tok-param">name</span> = <span class="tok-str">"custom_action"</span>
<span class="tok-param">version</span> = <span class="tok-str">"0.1.0"</span>
<span class="tok-param">edition</span> = <span class="tok-str">"2021"</span>

[<span class="tok-param">lib</span>]
<span class="tok-param">crate-type</span> = [<span class="tok-str">"cdylib"</span>]

[<span class="tok-param">dependencies</span>]
<span class="tok-param">core_types</span> = &#123; <span class="tok-param">path</span> = <span class="tok-str">"../../../core_types"</span> &#125;</code></pre></div>
          <p class="doc-p">Add <code>"actions/custom_pack/custom_action"</code> to the root Cargo workspace’s <code>members</code> list. <code>cdylib</code> tells Cargo to build a library that Morflow can load. The dependency path above points to the checkout’s shared <code>core_types</code> crate.</p>
          <p class="doc-p">Start with this complete pass-through action. It accepts a Tensor and returns it with the same shape. The four exported functions are the action’s public interface; <code>dimensions</code> is an ordinary Rust helper:</p>
          <div class="doc-code-card"><div class="doc-code-header"><span>src/lib.rs</span><span>rust</span></div><pre class="doc-code-pre"><code><span class="tok-comment">// actions/custom_pack/custom_action/src/lib.rs</span>
<span class="tok-keyword">use</span> core_types::&#123;<span class="tok-type">ActionArgs</span>, <span class="tok-type">DataType</span>, <span class="tok-type">InputDescriptor</span>, <span class="tok-type">Payload</span>, <span class="tok-type">PreparedData</span>,
                 <span class="tok-type">Shape</span>, <span class="tok-type">ShapeCheckResult</span>, <span class="tok-type">ShapeResult</span>&#125;;
<span class="tok-keyword">use</span> core_types::shapecheck::&#123;<span class="tok-keyword">self</span>, <span class="tok-type">PreparedArgs</span>&#125;;

<span class="tok-param">#[no_mangle]</span>
<span class="tok-keyword">pub</span> <span class="tok-keyword">extern</span> <span class="tok-str">"C"</span> <span class="tok-keyword">fn</span> <span class="tok-fn">get_input_type</span>() <span class="tok-op">-&gt;</span> <span class="tok-type">DataType</span> &#123; <span class="tok-type">DataType</span>::<span class="tok-type">Tensor</span> &#125;
<span class="tok-param">#[no_mangle]</span>
<span class="tok-keyword">pub</span> <span class="tok-keyword">extern</span> <span class="tok-str">"C"</span> <span class="tok-keyword">fn</span> <span class="tok-fn">get_output_type</span>() <span class="tok-op">-&gt;</span> <span class="tok-type">DataType</span> &#123; <span class="tok-type">DataType</span>::<span class="tok-type">Tensor</span> &#125;

<span class="tok-keyword">fn</span> <span class="tok-fn">dimensions</span>(input: <span class="tok-type">Shape</span>, _: <span class="tok-type">PreparedArgs</span>) <span class="tok-op">-&gt;</span> <span class="tok-type">ShapeResult</span> &#123;
    <span class="tok-type">ShapeResult</span>::<span class="tok-fn">Ok</span>(input)
&#125;

<span class="tok-param">#[no_mangle]</span>
<span class="tok-keyword">pub</span> <span class="tok-keyword">extern</span> <span class="tok-str">"C"</span> <span class="tok-keyword">fn</span> <span class="tok-fn">shapecheck</span>(input: <span class="tok-type">InputDescriptor</span>, args: <span class="tok-type">ActionArgs</span>) <span class="tok-op">-&gt;</span> <span class="tok-type">ShapeCheckResult</span> &#123;
    shapecheck::<span class="tok-fn">analyze</span>(input, args, <span class="tok-str">"custom_action"</span>, <span class="tok-fn">get_input_type</span>(),
                        <span class="tok-fn">get_output_type</span>(), <span class="tok-fn">Some</span>(dimensions), <span class="tok-type">None</span>)
&#125;

<span class="tok-param">#[no_mangle]</span>
<span class="tok-keyword">pub</span> <span class="tok-keyword">extern</span> <span class="tok-str">"C"</span> <span class="tok-keyword">fn</span> <span class="tok-fn">process</span>(payload: <span class="tok-type">Payload</span>, _prepared: <span class="tok-type">PreparedData</span>) <span class="tok-op">-&gt;</span> <span class="tok-type">Payload</span> &#123;
    <span class="tok-comment">// Pass-through example; replace with a transformation of the same shape.</span>
    payload
&#125;
</code></pre></div>
          <p class="doc-p">The engine requires all four exports: <code>get_input_type</code>, <code>get_output_type</code>, <code>shapecheck</code>, and <code>process</code>. Keep the shown signatures and <code>#[no_mangle]</code> attributes. To accept several payload kinds, combine their <code>DataType</code> values with <code>|</code>.</p>
        </div>
        <div id="custom-action-lifecycle" class="doc-section"><h3 class="doc-h3">5.2. How the Engine Runs an Action</h3>
          <ActionLifecycleDiagram phase="static" />
          <ActionLifecycleDiagram phase="runtime" />
          <div class="doc-table-container"><table class="doc-table"><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>When called</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td><code>get_input_type</code><br/><code>get_output_type</code></td><td>When the action is loaded.</td><td>Declare accepted and produced payload kinds.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>shapecheck(input, args)</code></td><td>During CLI checking with declared shapes; again before every execution with actual dimensions and metadata.</td><td>Check the input type, shape, and arguments. Describe the output shape and provide any data needed by processing.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>process(payload, prepared)</code></td><td>At runtime, only after a Ready result.</td><td>Read and transform the payload. Return the output promised by shapecheck, or <code>Payload::Error</code> with a useful message.</td></tr>
          </tbody></table></div>
          <p class="doc-p">During <code>morflow check</code>, <code>shapecheck</code> receives the declared shapes and available arguments. It predicts the output so Morflow can check the next action. <code>process</code> is not called at this stage.</p>
          <p class="doc-p">When the pipeline runs, Morflow calls <code>shapecheck</code> again with the actual input dimensions, metadata, and resolved arguments. A <code>Ready</code> result allows <code>process</code> to run. An <code>Invalid</code> or <code>Deferred</code> result stops that call and reports an error. After processing, Morflow checks that the returned payload matches the predicted type, shape, and Composite structure.</p>
          <p class="doc-p">The engine passes <code>prepared</code> automatically. Pipeline authors still write a normal call such as <code>custom_action</code> or <code>custom_action(axis=1)</code>. Prepared data from static checking is discarded; each runtime call gets fresh data, including calls inside parallel loops.</p>
        </div>
        <div id="custom-action-shapecheck" class="doc-section"><h3 class="doc-h3">5.3. Write the Shape Check</h3>
          <p class="doc-p"><code>shapecheck</code> receives two arguments. <code>InputDescriptor</code> describes the input’s type and shape, plus properties such as tensor dtype, image layout, and audio sample rate when available. <code>ActionArgs</code> contains positional and named arguments. The descriptor does not contain tensor elements, image pixels, audio samples, or encoded file contents.</p>
          <p class="doc-p">Use the same rules for static and runtime checking. Check ranks, compatible dimensions, component counts, axis bounds, and arguments that affect the operation. If a dimension or argument is unknown, preserve whatever output information you can still determine.</p>
          <div class="doc-table-container"><table class="doc-table"><thead><tr><th>Return value</th><th>When to use it</th><th>What Morflow does</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td><code>ShapeCheckResult::Ready</code></td><td>The available input information and arguments establish that the call is valid. Supply its output shape and prepared data.</td><td>Counts the static call as Verified. At runtime, passes the prepared data to processing.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ShapeCheckResult::Deferred</code></td><td>The call may be valid, but needs dimensions, metadata, or argument values that are not available yet. Supply the predicted output and unresolved reasons.</td><td>Shows Runtime check in the static report and carries the partial output shape forward.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ShapeCheckResult::Invalid</code></td><td>The known inputs or arguments are incompatible. Supply a specific reason.</td><td>Fails static checking or stops the runtime call before processing.</td></tr>
          </tbody></table></div>
          <p class="doc-p">For example, reshaping <code>Tensor[2,3]</code> to <code>Tensor[3,2]</code> has matching element counts. Reshaping it to <code>Tensor[4,2]</code> is invalid. With <code>Tensor[*,3]</code>, the input element count must be checked at runtime, but the requested output can still be described as <code>Tensor[3,2]</code>.</p>
          <p class="doc-p">In the pass-through example, <code>shapecheck::analyze</code> checks the accepted payload kind, calls <code>dimensions</code>, and creates the result and prepared data. Pass a single-shape helper as its <code>Some(dimensions)</code> argument, or a helper for a full Composite shape tree as its final argument. This helper suits actions whose validity depends on dimensions and arguments. Write a custom <code>shapecheck</code> body when you also need metadata or additional checks.</p>
          <p class="doc-p">Keep this function free of processing and side effects: it may run while a pipeline is only being inspected, and it may run concurrently. Give errors concrete explanations, such as “axis 3 is outside a rank-2 tensor” or “left columns 3 do not match right rows 4.”</p>
        </div>
        <div id="custom-action-processing" class="doc-section"><h3 class="doc-h3">5.4. Pass Checked Data to Processing</h3>
          <p class="doc-p"><code>process(payload, prepared)</code> receives the actual payload and the data returned by that call’s successful shape check. Put shape compatibility rules in <code>shapecheck</code>, then use the checked results here to perform the transformation.</p>
          <div class="doc-table-container"><table class="doc-table"><thead><tr><th>Prepared data</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td><code>prepared.output</code></td><td>The predicted output shape, including ordered Composite components.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>prepared.output_dims()</code></td><td>Get concrete dimensions for a single output when allocating or reshaping it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>prepared.args</code></td><td>Read positional or named arguments and reuse cached numeric parsing through <code>parse</code> and <code>usize_list</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>prepared.fields</code></td><td>Store action-specific results of checking, such as a normalized axis. Fields are named <code>PreparedValue</code> values; read them with <code>field</code>, <code>unsigned</code>, or <code>float</code>.</td></tr>
          </tbody></table></div>
          <p class="doc-p">A custom shape check returns <code>Ready</code> with a <code>PreparedData</code> containing the output prediction, <code>PreparedArgs::from(args)</code>, and any named fields. Use the same output prediction in both the result and the prepared data. <code>PreparedValue</code> supports integers, floating-point values, booleans, text, lists, and nested named fields.</p>
          <p class="doc-p">For an action with an axis argument, resolve negative indices and check bounds once in <code>shapecheck</code>, store the normalized axis in a field, and use that field in <code>process</code>. The pipeline author does not have to supply or manage this data.</p>
          <p class="doc-p">Checks that depend on actual contents still belong in processing: decoding a file, checking a numerical domain, or detecting a singular matrix. Return <code>Payload::Error("useful explanation".into())</code> if processing fails. Shape verification describes the call’s type and shape compatibility; it does not promise that every possible data value will succeed.</p>
        </div>
        <div id="custom-action-composite" class="doc-section"><h3 class="doc-h3">5.5. Handle Composite Inputs and Outputs</h3>
          <p class="doc-p">A Composite holds an ordered list of payloads. Its shape description is also an ordered list, and can contain nested Composites. An action that accepts Composite inputs should declare <code>DataType::Composite</code>, check the component count and each component’s kind, then read their shapes in order.</p>
          <p class="doc-p">Use <code>input.value.get(0)</code> for the first component and <code>input.value.get(1)</code> for the second. These return optional references so you can report a missing component clearly. Metadata uses the same ordering through <code>input.metadata.get(index)</code>. In processing, match <code>Payload::Composite(items)</code> and use the corresponding indices.</p>
          <p class="doc-p">For matrix multiplication, component 0 is the left operand and component 1 is the right operand. Check their shared inner dimension and predict the result’s outer dimensions. For an action that returns several results, return <code>ValueShape::Composite</code> from checking and <code>Payload::Composite</code> from processing in the same order.</p>
          <p class="doc-p">For example, QR returns Q first and R second. Describing both output shapes lets a pipeline select <code>$parts[0]</code> or <code>$parts[1]</code> and have downstream actions checked against the selected component’s shape.</p>
        </div>
        <div id="custom-action-package" class="doc-section"><h3 class="doc-h3">5.6. Build, Package &amp; Import</h3>
          <p class="doc-p">Build the action and package the resulting library into an action cache. The packaging tool reads the action name and version from Cargo.toml and the pack name from its parent folder. Run these commands from the Morflow repository root on Linux:</p>
          <div class="doc-code-card"><div class="doc-code-header"><span>Local action installation</span><span>shell</span></div><pre class="doc-code-pre"><code><span class="tok-fn">cargo</span> build --release -p custom_action
<span class="tok-fn">python3</span> scripts/package_action.py actions/custom_pack/custom_action/Cargo.toml \
  target/release/libcustom_action.so --cache ./actions_cache
<span class="tok-keyword">export</span> MORFLOW_ACTIONS_PATH=<span class="tok-str">"$PWD/actions_cache"</span></code></pre></div>
          <div class="doc-code-card"><div class="doc-code-header"><span>Use your custom action</span><span>morf</span></div><pre class="doc-code-pre"><code><span class="tok-keyword">from</span> custom_pack/<span class="tok-num">0.1</span>.<span class="tok-num">0</span> <span class="tok-keyword">import</span> <span class="tok-fn">custom_action</span>
<span class="tok-keyword">accept</span> <span class="tok-type">Tensor[2,3]</span> <span class="tok-var">$input</span>
<span class="tok-var">$input</span> <span class="tok-op">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="tok-fn">custom_action</span> <span class="tok-op">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="tok-fn">emit</span></code></pre></div>
          <p class="doc-p">On macOS, use <code>target/release/libcustom_action.dylib</code>; on Windows, use <code>target/release/custom_action.dll</code> and set <code>MORFLOW_ACTIONS_PATH</code> using your shell’s environment-variable syntax. Packaging creates the versioned binary, checksum receipt, and offline action catalog. If you add a <code>SPEC.md</code> beside Cargo.toml, it is packaged with the action too.</p>
          <p class="doc-p">The Linux cache entry is <code>actions_cache/custom_pack/custom_action_action-0.1.0-linux-x86_64.so</code>, with its receipt at the same path plus <code>.json</code>. Import the packaged version explicitly, as above. Morflow loads from the local cache without network access. After changing the action, rebuild and package it, then reload the pipeline to use the new binary.</p>
        </div>
        <div id="custom-action-testing" class="doc-section"><h3 class="doc-h3">5.7. Test the Action</h3>
          <p class="doc-p">Save the import example as <code>custom_pipeline.morf</code>. Check it with the same action cache you will use for execution:</p>
          <div class="doc-code-card"><div class="doc-code-header"><span>Check your pipeline</span><span>shell</span></div><pre class="doc-code-pre"><code><span class="tok-fn">cargo</span> test -p custom_action
<span class="tok-fn">cargo</span> run -p pipeline -- check custom_pipeline.morf</code></pre></div>
          <p class="doc-p">For runtime unit tests, call <code>core_types::shapecheck::execute("custom_action", shapecheck, process, payload)</code>. This runs the same shape-check, processing, and output-verification sequence as the engine. Calling <code>process</code> directly only tests processing and requires you to supply prepared data yourself.</p>
          <p class="doc-p">Cover valid concrete shapes, incompatible shapes, and dynamic declarations. Check that invalid inputs are rejected before processing, that partial shapes retain any known rank and dimensions, and that runtime outputs match the prediction. For Composite actions, also test missing components, incorrect kinds, and component ordering.</p>
          <p class="doc-p">Finally, load the example pipeline through a binding and run it with a matching Tensor. Try <code>Tensor[*,3]</code> in the declaration as well: the static report should identify unresolved dimensions, while execution checks the actual dimensions supplied by the host.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
